{
  "dom_id": "step-q216060",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $5$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} & |\\times 5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1 & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+3b=9 \\\\ 3a+b=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =7 \\\\ b & =7-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $b=7-3a$ into the first equation and then solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+3b & =9 \\\\ 5a+3(7-3a) & =9 \\\\ 5a+21-9a & =9 \\\\ -4a & =-12 \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $b,$ we substitute $a=3$ into the equation $b=7-3a$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =7-3a \\\\  & =7-3(3) \\\\  & =7-9 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3$ and $b=-2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
